86410921 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410931 1079 #uuid "9801aa3c-15d6-5b0a-98a6-d929fbad53c7" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410904 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410908 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410924 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410926 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410927 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1077 "selection" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410927 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410932 1079 #uuid "af3928a6-40a7-5b56-812b-bbcc73b73c7b" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410907 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410913 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410914 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410923 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410925 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1077 "selection" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410925 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410933 1079 #uuid "b94f51c3-8089-500c-bb22-065f6d87882e" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410929 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1069 "q-82304" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1071 "![](images/ef/ef3933fc56e981bd0ef6e88754c46f5ab00b15230cce5fc2ea224ecf7c1f8f0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1072 #uuid "00868f73-2a29-5bc5-b5fa-287f2fcfd175" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410934 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/f0/f020379f306770bd87fc499ac1fef238dc6aa639fb36d5229502fcd7dd455610.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410933 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1069 "q-170667" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=2x$\n- $h:Z\\to Z$ defined by $h(x)=2x$" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1072 #uuid "6f4dc780-0892-5640-8a66-1c76dbdf69ef" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410935 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This means that \n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=4.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 2x & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{2})=2(\\frac{y}{2})=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Notice that if $x$ is an integer number, then $h(x)=2x$ is an even number. This means that the range of $h$ is the set of even numbers.\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in Z$ such that $h(x)=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410932 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1069 "q-105321" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1071 "![](images/63/63032bf65ec10d55f9a0b968a7179db934ad6f17b869d50999e4a0f710be9977.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(-1,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1072 #uuid "80ecff06-aa66-5d3f-b38e-164bc959ec2c" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410936 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/71/71bd13b2dd618fb60cd2e7f6c2e3807f3449e233ccb9b60e1cbd14cc928345b3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410930 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1069 "q-77480" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:Z\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)={2}^{x}$" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1072 #uuid "f181e810-d779-5077-8c9b-e3a1b446edcd" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410937 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ x+1 & =y \\\\ x & =y-1\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nf(x)=f(y-1)=(y-1)+1=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is both surjective and injective, $f$ is bijective.    \n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=2.$ Then there is no rational number $x$  such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=0.$ Then there is no integer $x$  such that $g(x)=0,$  because ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410931 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1069 "q-82572" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1071 "![](images/ce/ce68b2f27b88bd43d6eebfa5380a22c00e15e4194e0ad45e6778f761667a3ceb.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x-2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1072 #uuid "f636c031-3e1e-5fb8-bd29-acadcf4da72e" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410938 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/2f/2ff4c0183b91aa23d780f665aa0f038b374583e9f24e245951c4c69ea5f2e5b8.png)\n\n           \n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=-2\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410939 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410940 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410939 1062 #uuid "927269ee-7d40-5f59-9c03-e0f6ef8e36d9" 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410939 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410937 13194139534123 17592186045417 true] [17592186410940 10